Manage Non-shared Storage with StatefulSets
Objective
Deploy applications that scale but do NOT share storage.
Examples of such applications:
•	MySQL
•	Cassandra
•	MongoDB
•	Kafka
•	Elasticsearch
These applications need separate storage for each instance.
________________________________________
1. Application Clustering
Many enterprise applications run in clusters.
Example:
MySQL Cluster
   ├── DB Node 1
   ├── DB Node 2
   └── DB Node 3
Each node:
•	Processes requests
•	Stores data
•	Synchronizes with other nodes
Important requirement:
Each node must have its own persistent storage
Because sharing storage may cause:
•	Data corruption
•	Transaction conflicts
•	Performance issues
________________________________________
2. File Storage (NAS)
File storage provides a directory-based storage system.
Example:
/data/files
/data/backups
/data/images
Typical storage type:
NAS (Network Attached Storage)
NAS Characteristics
Feature	Description
Directory structure	Yes
Shared storage	Yes
Easy management	Yes
Performance	Moderate
________________________________________
NAS Protocols
Two common protocols:
NFS (Network File System)
Allows remote systems to mount file systems.
Example:
server:/shared-data
Pods can mount it as:
/var/www/html
________________________________________
SMB (Server Message Block)
Used in Windows environments.
Used for:
•	Shared folders
•	Network printers
•	File servers
________________________________________
File Storage Use Cases
Good for:
•	Web servers
•	File sharing
•	Backup systems
•	FTP servers
•	Archiving
Example:
Web Servers
     ↓
Shared NFS Storage
     ↓
HTML files
________________________________________
Limitation of File Storage
File storage is not ideal for heavy workloads.
Example problems:
•	Big data
•	High database transactions
•	Large unstructured data
In those cases block storage is better.
________________________________________
3. Block Storage
Block storage provides raw storage devices.
Example:
Disk /dev/sda
Disk /dev/sdb
Applications must:
1.	Format disk
2.	Mount disk
3.	Store data
________________________________________
Technologies used
Examples:
•	SAN (Storage Area Network)
•	iSCSI
•	Cloud disks (AWS EBS)
________________________________________
Block Storage Characteristics
Feature	Description
Access	Raw block level
Performance	High
Latency	Low
Ideal for	Databases
________________________________________
Block Storage Use Cases
Best for:
•	SQL databases
•	Virtual machines
•	Data processing
•	RAID configurations
Example:
Database Pod
     ↓
Block Volume
     ↓
SAN Storage
________________________________________
4. Stateful Applications
A stateful application remembers previous state.
Example:
Bank system
Transaction example:
Balance = 1000
Withdraw = 200
Balance = 800
The application must remember previous transactions.
Examples of Stateful Apps:
•	MySQL
•	PostgreSQL
•	MongoDB
•	Cassandra
•	Redis cluster
________________________________________
5. Deployments vs StatefulSets
Deployment (Stateless)
Pods are identical and interchangeable.
Example:
Web Server Deployment

pod-abc
pod-def
pod-xyz
Characteristics:
•	Random pod names
•	Shared storage
•	Easy scaling
________________________________________
StatefulSet (Stateful)
Pods have stable identity and storage.
Example:
dbserver-0
dbserver-1
dbserver-2
Characteristics:
•	Fixed pod names
•	Unique storage
•	Stable network identity
________________________________________
StatefulSet Key Concept
Each pod gets its own PVC
Example:
dbserver-0 → pvc-dbserver-0
dbserver-1 → pvc-dbserver-1
dbserver-2 → pvc-dbserver-2
Pods do not share storage.
________________________________________
6. Headless Services
StatefulSets require Headless Service.
A normal service:
ClusterIP Service
Example:
service IP → load balances traffic
________________________________________
Headless Service
A headless service has:
clusterIP: None
Example YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: mysql
spec:
  clusterIP: None
  selector:
    app: database
  ports:
  - port: 3306
________________________________________
Why Headless Service?
DNS returns IP of each pod directly.
Example:
dbserver-0.mysql
dbserver-1.mysql
dbserver-2.mysql
Instead of load balancing.
________________________________________
7. StatefulSet YAML Example
Example:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: dbserver
spec:
  replicas: 3
  serviceName: mysql
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: database

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: database
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: database
        image: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/mysql-app:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3306
        volumeMounts:
        - name: data
          mountPath: /var/lib/mysql

  volumeClaimTemplates:
  - metadata:
      name: data
    spec:
      accessModes:
        - ReadWriteOncePod
      storageClassName: lvms-vg1
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 1Gi
________________________________________
8. Create Headless Service
oc create -f headless-service.yml
Check service:
oc get svc
Output:
mysql   ClusterIP   None
This confirms headless service created.
________________________________________
9. Create StatefulSet
oc create -f statefulset-dbserver.yml
Check:
oc get statefulset
Example output:
dbserver   3/3
________________________________________
10. Pods Created
oc get pods
Example:
dbserver-0
dbserver-1
dbserver-2
Important:
Pods are created one by one in order.
dbserver-0 → first
dbserver-1 → second
dbserver-2 → third
________________________________________
11. Stable DNS Names
Each pod gets predictable DNS.
Format:
<pod-name>.<service-name>
Example:
dbserver-0.mysql
dbserver-1.mysql
dbserver-2.mysql
________________________________________
12. Each Pod Gets Separate PVC
Run:
oc get pvc
Example:
data-dbserver-0
data-dbserver-1
data-dbserver-2
Storage mapping:
dbserver-0 → data-dbserver-0
dbserver-1 → data-dbserver-1
dbserver-2 → data-dbserver-2
Each pod stores its own data.
________________________________________
13. Check PVC used by Pod
Example:
oc describe pod dbserver-0
Output:
ClaimName: data-dbserver-0
Each pod mounts its own PVC.
________________________________________
14. Scaling StatefulSet
Increase replicas:
oc scale statefulset/dbserver --replicas 4
New pod created:
dbserver-3
Also creates:
data-dbserver-3 PVC
________________________________________
15. Delete StatefulSet
oc delete statefulset dbserver
Important behavior:
Pods deleted
PVCs NOT deleted
Example:
data-dbserver-0
data-dbserver-1
data-dbserver-2
Data remains safe.
________________________________________
Deployment vs StatefulSet (Important Exam Table)
Feature	Deployment	StatefulSet
Pod Identity	Random	Stable
Pod Naming	Random hash	Ordered
Storage	Shared	Separate
Scaling	Parallel	Ordered
Use case	Stateless apps	Databases
________________________________________
